-- title:
--   Relative entropy between push-forward measures
-- statement:
--   Observing a random element and restricting to the $\sigma$-algebra it generates are the same thing, as far as relative entropy is concerned.
--
--   Let $\mu,\nu$ be finite measures on $(\Omega,\mathcal F)$ and let $Z:\Omega\to\mathcal Z$ be measurable. Write $\mu_Z$ and $\nu_Z$ for the push-forwards, $\mu_Z(U)=\mu(Z\in U)$, and $\sigma(Z)$ for the $\sigma$-algebra generated by $Z$. Then
--
--   $$
--   D\big(\mu_Z\,\|\,\nu_Z\big) \;=\; D\big(\mu|_{\sigma(Z)}\,\|\,\nu|_{\sigma(Z)}\big).
--   $$
--
--   Both sides measure exactly the information about the hypothesis $\mu$ versus $\nu$ that is carried by the observation $Z$: on the left it is packaged as a measure on the observation space, on the right as a measure on the original space seen through a coarser $\sigma$-algebra.
--
--   The identity is the standard bridge between the two ways of stating a data-processing or change-of-measure argument, and it is what lets one move a divergence computation from an abstract filtered space to a concrete product space where a chain rule is available.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\sigma(Z)$ is `MeasurableSpace.comap`, and the restriction is `Measure.trim`. The proof needs no separability or standard-Borel assumption: every $\sigma(Z)$-measurable set is by definition a preimage, which is all that is used. The density on $\sigma(Z)$ is the pull-back $\frac{d\mu_Z}{d\nu_Z}\circ Z$.
-- source:
--   Lattimore and Szepesvari, Bandit Algorithms (CUP 2020), https://tor-lattimore.com/downloads/book/book.pdf : Exercise 14.9 (Relative entropy between push-forward measures), printed p. 195.

import Mathlib.InformationTheory.KullbackLeibler.Basic

open MeasureTheory InformationTheory Set
open scoped ENNReal

theorem InformationTheory.klDiv_map_eq_klDiv_trim_comap {α β : Type*}
    {mα : MeasurableSpace α} {mβ : MeasurableSpace β}
    {φ : α → β} (hφ : Measurable φ) (μ ν : Measure α)
    [IsFiniteMeasure μ] [IsFiniteMeasure ν] :
    klDiv (μ.map φ) (ν.map φ) =
      @klDiv α (mβ.comap φ) (μ.trim hφ.comap_le) (ν.trim hφ.comap_le) := by
  sorry
